import sys; sys.path.insert(0,'/home/claude/work'); from gen import *
W='/tmp/tlv-src/apps/web'; A='https://apocalypse12.com'
make(19,f"This is a summary. The Fathers on chapter 19 are set out in full on The Woman and the Ark: [The Rider Called Faithful and True]({A}/the-rider-called-faithful-and-true) and [The Beast and the False Prophet Taken]({A}/the-beast-and-the-false-prophet-taken).",
[('/topics/revelation-and-the-mass','Revelation and the Mass'),(f'{A}/the-rider-called-faithful-and-true','The Rider Called Faithful and True (The Woman and the Ark)')],
[('19:7–9','The wedding feast of the Lamb',[
 ('Betrothal now, marriage then.','Oecumenius: in this age the Church is betrothed, as St. Paul told the Corinthians (2 Corinthians 11:2), and holds the Spirit as the pledge.'),
 ('The fine linen.','Apringius of Beja and St. Bede both stress that it is no outward finery; it is the righteous deeds of the saints.'),
 ('Those invited.','Apringius hears the Lord at the Last Supper, who will drink the fruit of the vine new in his Father’s kingdom (Matthew 26:29).'),
]),
('19:11–13','The rider on the white horse',[
 ('The rider is Christ,','for every Father cited. The horse is the body he took (Apringius, Bede), or his saints, on whom he rides as he rode on Paul (Oecumenius, Acts 9:15). Origen reads the vision of the soul: heaven is open to those who bear the image of the heavenly man (1 Corinthians 15:49).'),
 ('Many diadems.','Origen: if the lie were one, one crown would do. The lies that pass for truth are many, and the Word has overcome each.'),
 ('The name no one knows.','St. Andrew of Caesarea: the hidden name is the incomprehensibility of his being. From his works he has many names, such as Shepherd, Light and Life; what he is in himself is known to the Trinity alone.'),
]),
('19:17–18','The angel in the sun and God’s supper',[
 ('The angel.','Apringius: Michael, who stands up for the people (Daniel 12:1). Primasius, St. Caesarius of Arles and Bede, following Tyconius: the Church’s preaching, now in the open.'),
 ('Two suppers.','Andrew sets the wedding feast beside this one. God’s first will is that all be saved (1 Timothy 2:4); each one chooses his supper by his works.'),
]),
('19:19–21','The beast and the false prophet taken',[
 ('Seized, not fought.','The chapter tells of no battle. The two are simply taken. The sister site gives the Fathers’ readings of who the two captives are, and where they differ.'),
]),
],f'{W}/src/data/fathers/chapter-19.tsx'); wire(19,W)
make(20,f"This is a summary. The Fathers on chapter 20 are set out in full on The Woman and the Ark: [The Dragon Bound]({A}/the-dragon-bound) and [Gog and Magog and the Dragon’s End]({A}/gog-and-magog-and-the-dragons-end), which includes a note on the word “predestined.”",
[('/topics/the-millennium','The Millennium'),('/topics/gog-and-magog','Gog and Magog'),(f'{A}/the-dragon-bound','The Dragon Bound (The Woman and the Ark)')],
[('20:1–3','The dragon bound',[
 ('Who binds him.','Primasius: the Lord himself, the stronger one who came to bind the strong man (Matthew 12:29). St. Andrew of Caesarea: an angel carrying out Christ’s sentence.'),
 ('When.','At the Passion, says Andrew. The demons who begged not to be sent into the abyss already knew it.'),
 ('The abyss.','St. Augustine: the hearts of the godless, where the devil is shut in more deeply the more he is shut out of the hearts of believers.'),
]),
('20:4–6','The thousand years',[
 ('The time of the Church.','St. Augustine allows two readings, and in both the thousand years are the present age of the Church, in which the saints already reign with Christ (*City of God* 20). Andrew refuses a literal thousand: the number means many, or complete. St. Caesarius of Arles counts it from the Passion.'),
 ('A future reign on earth.','St. Victorinus, among the earliest, expects a first resurrection of the just and a thousand-year reign before the last judgment. The Church has not accepted this reading (Catechism of the Catholic Church 676).'),
 ('Others.','Oecumenius stands alone in taking the thousand years for the days of the Lord’s earthly life. Apringius of Beja finds the cross in the number itself.'),
]),
('20:7–10','Gog and Magog',[
 ('No nation on a map.','Augustine: they are not some barbarian tribe in one corner of the earth. The camp of the saints is the Church spread through all nations, hemmed in everywhere and never giving up her fight.'),
 ('The fire from heaven.','Augustine: the firmness of the saints, given by God’s grace. Caesarius: the fire of the Spirit, which brings some to faith, or the fire of their own sins. Andrew: real fire, or the glorious coming of Christ (2 Thessalonians 2:8).'),
]),
('20:11–15','The judgment and the books',[
 ('The dead, great and small.','The sister site sets out the Fathers on the great white throne, the books, and the second death, with a note explaining how the Church understands predestination: God predestines no one to hell (Catechism of the Catholic Church 1037).'),
]),
],f'{W}/src/data/fathers/chapter-20.tsx'); wire(20,W)
